rwsale.com 105. Trantraveda.org \n99. Clickbd.com 106. Snapdeal.com \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |461", "t": "Introduction of Rajdhani Train", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 461 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nINTRODUCTION OF RAJDHANI TRAIN \n \n†461. SHRI CHANDRAKANT KHAIRE: \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has not introduced any superfast or \nRajdhani train between Delhi and Aurangabad for the facility of \ntourists coming from various parts of the world keeping in view the \nworld famous Ajanta caves situated at Aurangabad;  \n \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(c) the reaction of the Government thereto? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n (a) to (c)  Aurangabad station is presently connected to New Delhi \nby one pair of superfast train viz. 12715/12716 Amritsar -H.S. Nanded \nExpress (daily).  Introduction of new superfast train from \nAurangabad to New Delhi is not feasible, at present, due to \noperational and resource constraints.   \n            Rajdhani Express trains are long distance fast moving \nprestigious trains primarily meant for connecting National capital \nand State capitals, running on very tight timings and with limited \nhalts enroute.   As such, at present there is no proposal to introduce \nRajdhani Express train from New Delhi to Aurangabad. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |462", "t": "Independent Channel", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 462 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nIndependent Channel \n \n462.   SHRI B.N. CHANDRAPPA: \n           \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to create an independent \nchannel without any advertisements during the telecast of cricket \nmatches and if so, the details thereof; \n  \n(b) whether the Government has taken steps to consult various \nstakeholders in this regard; and \n \n(c) if so, the time by which the independent channel is likely to be \nintroduced? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR YOUTH AFFAIRS \nAND SPORTS \n(SHRI VIJEY GOEL) \n \n \n(a)  Madam, Ministry of Information & Broadcasting has informed that \nthere  is no such proposal to create an independent channel without any  \nadvertisements during the telecast of cricket matches. \n(b)&(c)  Does not arise.  \n \n*****\n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |463", "t": "Demographic Profile of Minorities", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †463 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nDEMOGRAPHIC PROFILE OF MINORITIES \n \n†463. SHRI  VISHNU  DAYAL  RAM: \nSHRI  RAM  CHARITRA  NISHAD: \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has conducted any survey regarding \ndemographic profile of minorities in the country during the last three years \nand the current year; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the number of such surveys conducted by the \nprevious Governments and the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has any proposal to increase funds to be provided \nfor development of minorities on priority basis in the districts having \nminority population; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINORITY AFFAIRS (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) & (b): No, Madam. \n \n(c) & (d): As against the Budget provision of Rs. 3,712.78 crore provided during \n2015-16, Rs. 3,6 32.26 Crore (provisional data) was expended by the Minis try of \nMinority Affairs for the welfare of minorities. For the year 2016 -17, t he \nGovernment has increased the Budget provision for the Ministry at Rs. 3,800.00 \ncrore.  \n \n \n* * * * * "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |464", "t": "Towers in Residential Areas", "head": " \n  \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.464  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20 TH  JULY, 2016 \n \nTOWERS IN RESIDENTIAL AREAS \n \n464.  SHRI PONGULETI SRINIVASA REDDY:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the National Human Rights Commission h as issued notice to the \nGovernment in the wake of allegations in a complaint that mobile towers in the vicinity \nof residential areas, emit radiations that are hazardous to human health; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  the strategy/measures the Government is adopti ng/planning to adopt to ensure \nthat no substantive damage is caused to the life an d health of human and other living \nbeings; \n \n(d)  whether as per Government order dated 21.08.20 13, the local selfcGovernment \ninstitutions should confirm that the proposed tower s do not come within 500 m of \nschools/hospitals, but it does not cover residentia l areas and due to this order mobile \noperators are violating the safe radiation range an d are erecting towers in thickly \npopulated residential areas; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and the measures ta ken by the Government to \naddress the problem? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS & \n MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a) & (b)  Yes, Madam. Law Division of National Hum an Rights Commission \n(NHRC) has issued a notice dated 02c06c2016 in case no. 233/90/0/2016 to Ministry \nof Communication and Information Technology in resp ect of a complaint regarding \nimpact of EMF emission from mobile towers on health . A suitable reply to the notice \nhas been submitted to the Commission inter alia bringing out the following:  \n \n “With regard to impact of Electromagnetic Field (E MF) emissions from mobile \ntowers on health, World Health Organization (WHO) h as referred to approximately \n25,000 articles published around the world over pas t 30 years, and based on an inc\ndepth review of scientific literature, has stated, “ Despite the feeling of some people \nthat more research needs to be done, scientific knowledge in this area is now more \nextensive than for most chemicals.” WHO has concluded c “current evidence does not \nconfirm the existence of any health consequences from exposure to low level \nelectromagnetic fields ”.   \n \nWHO in its Fact Sheet No. 304, May 2006 on Electrom agnetic Fields and \nPublic Health (Base Stations and Wireless Technolog ies) has concluded that “ From \nall evidence accumulated so far, no adverse short or long term health effects have \nbeen shown to occur from the RF Signals produced by base stations kelipsis.. considering \nthe very low exposure levels and research results collected to date, there is no \nconvincing scientific evidence that the weak RF Signals from base stations and \nwireless networks caused adverse health effects. ” Also, in September 2013, WHO in \nonline question and answers have mentioned that \" Studies to date provide no \nindication that environmental exposure to RF fields, such as from base stations, \nincreases the risk of cancer or any other disease.\"   \n \nContdkelipsis2/c \n\n \n  \n \n \n \nc2c \n \nFurther, several High Courts viz Hon’ble High Court s of Punjab & Haryana, \nMadras, Kerala, Gujarat, Delhi, Himachal Pradesh an d Allahabad have dismissed \npetitions, where the mobile tower installations wer e challenged on grounds of health \neffects of EMF radiations at various locations including residential. \n \n(c)  Department of Telecommunications (DoT) have be en monitoring global \ndevelopments and has already taken necessary steps and adopted stricter norms for \nsafety from EMF emmissions from mobile towers. Gove rnment of India has been \ntaking due precautions and necessary actions in res pect of EMF radiation emitted \nfrom mobile towers through various guidelines & nor ms issued from time to time \ntaking into account the international standards/nor ms. Presently the norms for \nexposure limit for the Radio Frequency Field (Base Station Emissions) in India are \nalready 10 times more stringent than the safe limit s prescribed by International \nCommission on Non Ionizing Radiation Protection (IC NIRP) and recommended by \nWHO. Further, Government of India has taken adequat e steps to ensure that \nTelecommunications Service Providers strictly adhere to these prescribed norms. \n  \nAll new mobile Base Transmitting Stations (BTS) sit es start radiating \ncommercially only after ensuring adherence to prescribed norms and after submission \nof self certificate to this effect to relevant Tele com Enforcement Resource & \nMonitoring (TERM) field offices of DoT. In order to  ensure compliance to the \nprescribed stricter precautionary norms of EMF radi ation from mobile tower, the \nextensive audit of compliance of selfccertificates being submitted by telecom service \nproviders and Base Transceiver Station (BTS) sites is carried out by TERM field"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |465", "t": "Bio-Toilets", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.465 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nBIO-TOILETS  \n \n465. SHRI ABHISHEK SINGH: \n         SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARSWATI: \n   \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS  be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of trains which have eco-friendly bio-toilet facility;  \n(b) whether any target has been set by the Government to provide \ngreen toilets in all trains and if so, the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor; and \n(c) the  details of  funds that has been allocated for the above \npurpose in fiscal year, 2016-17? \n  \n ANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n( SHRI MANOJ SINHA ) \n \n(a)  to  (c)   A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n***** \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 465 BY SHRI ABHISHEK SINGH AND \nSHRI SUMEDHANAND SARSWATI  TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA \nON 20.07.2016  REGARDING BIO-TOILETS \n  \n \n(a) Bio-toilets, based on the indigenous design and technology \ndeveloped in association with Defence Research & Development \nOrganisation, are being proliferated in coaches by Indian Railways \nand there are about 800 trains which have eco -friendly bio -toilet \nfacility. Some of these trains, in which bio -toilets have been \nintroduced on more than 50% of coaches, are indicated below: \nRailway Trains \nCENTRAL  12140/12139 Sewagram Express, 11015/11016 \nKushi Nagar Express, 12105/12106 Vidhrabha \nExpress \nEAST \nCOAST  \n22871/22872 Bhubaneswar -Tirupati Express, \n12830/12829 Bhubaneswar -Chennai Express, \n12843/12844 Puri -Ahmedabad Express, \n18507/18508 Hirakud Express, 18518/18517 \nVisakhapatnam-Korba Express \nEAST \nCENTRAL  \n15227/15228 Muzaffarpur -Yesvantpur Express, \n12567/12568 Rajya Rani Express, 13301/13302 \nDhanbad-Tatanagar Suvarna Rekha Express \nEASTERN  12345/12346 Saraighat Express, 12311/12312 Kalka \nMail, 12307/12308 Jodhpur Express, 12343/12344 \nSealdah-Darjeeling Mail, 12377/12378 Padatik \nExpress  \nNORTH \nCENTRAL  \n12403/12404 Allahabad -Jaipur Express, \n12451/12452 Shram Shakti Express, 11107/11108 \nBundelkhand Express, 11125/11126 Gwalior -Indore \nExpress \nNORTH \nEASTERN  \n12581/12582 Manduadih -New Delhi Express, \n12533/12534 Pushpak Express, 12555/12556 \nGorakhdham Express, 15007/15008 Krishak \nExpress               \n    \n  \n-2/- \n\n \n-2- \n \nNORTHEAST \nFRONTIER  \n15648/15647 Guwahati-Lokmanya Tilak (T) Express, \n15618/15617 Naharlagun -Guwahati Intercity \nExpress, 22511/22512 Lokmanya Tilak (T) -Kamkhya \nKarambhoomi Express \nNORTHERN  12429/12430 Lucknow -New Delhi AC Express, \n22418/22417 Mahamana Express, 14215/14216 \nGanga Gomti Express, 14008/14007 Sadbhavana \nExpress \nNORTH \nWESTERN  \n12458/12457 Bikaner -Delhi Sarai Rohilla Express,   \n12968/12967 Jaipur -Chennai Express, 12976/1 2975 \nJaipur-Mysuru Express, 12996/12995 Udaipur City -\nBandra (T) Express \nSOUTH \nCENTRAL  \n17016/17015 Visakha Express, 17018/17017 Rajkot \nExpress, 12702/12701 Hussain Sagar Express, \n17652/17651 Chennai Egmore -Kacheguda Express, \n17037/17038 Bikaner Express \nSOUTH \nEAST \nCENTRAL  \n \n18239-12855 Bilaspur -Nagpur Shivnath Express, \n58806 Gondia Passenger \nSOUTH \nEASTERN  \n12837/12838 Howrah -Puri Express,  12863/12864 \nHowrah-Yesvantpur Express, 12810/12809 Howrah -\nMumbai Mail   \nSOUTHERN  12637/12638 Pandiyan Express, 16101/16102 \nChennai Egmore -Rameswaram Express, \n16603/16604 Maveli Express, 16714/16713 \nRameswaram-Chennai Egmore Express, \n16723/16724 Ananthapuri Express, 16733/16734 \nRameswaram-Okha Express, 16780/16779 \nRameswaram-Tirupati Express \nSOUTH \nWESTERN  \n12627/12628 Karnataka Express,  12657/12658 \nBangalore Chennai Mail, 16525/16526 Bangalore - \nKanyakumari Express, 12253/12254 Yesv antpur-\nBhagalpur Anga Express \n.  \n.  \n -3/- \n\n \n-3- \nWEST \nCENTRAL  \n22981/22982 Kota -Sri Ganga Nagar Express, \n12121/12122 Madhya Pradesh Sampark Kranti \nExpress, 12191/12192 Shridham Express, \n12155/12156 Shane-E-Bhopal Express, 11449/11450 \nJabalpur-Sri Mata Vaishno Devi Katra Express  \nWESTERN  19005/19006 Mumbai Central -Okha Saurashtra \nExpress, 59547/59548 Okha Passenger, \n19216/19215 Mumbai Central -Porbandar Saurashtra \nExpress, 19269/19270 Porbandar -Muzaffarpur \nExpress \n \nIn addition to the above trains, coaches fitted with bio -toilets are \nrunning in other trains too in piecemeal manner. These trains are \nbeing progressively augmented with coaches equipped with bio -\ntoilets. \n \n(b)  Yes, Madam.  It  is planned to fit bio-toilets in the entire fleet of \nIndian Railways’ coaches by the year 2021-22. \n(c)  Funds of  ₹395 Crores have been allocated for the above \npurpose in fiscal year 2016-17. \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |466", "t": "Production of Titanium Metal", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nDEPARTMENT OF ATOMIC ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 466 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nPRODUCTION OF TITANIUM METAL \n \n466.  SHRI  P. KUMAR: \n \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has any plan to start t he production of titanium metal \n and other value added materials from Rare Earths; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the location where the Rare Earth Units are pr esently functioning in the \n country and the quantity of products produced from  these units? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR, PERSONNEL, PUBLIC GRIEVA NCES AND \nPENSIONS AND PRIME MINISTER'S OFFICE (DR. JITENDRA SINGH): \n \n(a) No, Sir. \n \n(b) Does not arise in view of the reply at (a) abov e.  \n \n(c) The Location of Rare Earth Plant in India owned  by Indian Rare Earths \nLimited (IREL), a Public Sector Undertaking under t he Department of Atomic \nEnergy (DAE), is at Aluva, in Kerala.  The Plant, r efurbished for production of \nhigh pure rare earth, has been commissioned and com mercial production will \nstart during year 2016-17.   \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |467", "t": "Biometric Details for Visa", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.467 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nBIOMETRIC DETAILS FOR VISA \n \n467.      SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA: \n     \nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has rolled out a proje ct which ensures \ncollection of applicant’s biometric details before any kind of visa is \nissued; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and whether out of the 178 Indian missions, \nonly 55 have been equipped with this biometric recording facility; \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether a proposal was floated to have the biometrics of the tourists \non arrival in India and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) whether the Government has taken a final call on enforcing  the \nbiometric aspect in the visa issuing process  and if so, the details \nthereof?  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n(a) to (e) The Ministry of Home Affairs has implemented a Mission Mode \nProject on Immigration, Visa and Foreigners Registration & Tracking \n(IVFRT). This Project is a multi -Ministry project which brings together a \nnumber of immigration, visa and registration tasks on to one IT platform, for \ngreater control and efficiency. The project also has provision for biometric \nenrolment and authentication. \n \nAs on date, out of our 178 Missions/Posts abroad, biometric enrolment \nsystem has been implemented in 79 Indian Missions/Posts. A list of these 79 \nIndian Missions/Posts abroad is attached as Annexure-I. \n \nThe Government of India launched the e-Tourist Visa (e-TV) facility on \nNovember 27, 2014 with a view to simplify the visa process for foreign \ntourists visiting India. The scheme has since been extended to 150 \ncountries. A list of such co untries is attached as Annexure -II. Biometric \ndetails of the foreigners visiting India on e-Tourist Visas (e-TV) are captured \non arrival at 16 designated Indian airports. \n***** \n\nANNEXURE-I \nBiometric Implemented Missions \n \nS.NO Country Name Mission Name S.NO Country Name Mission Name \n1 AFGANISTAN KANDHAR 41 NETHERLANDS THE HAGUE \n2 AFGANISTAN JALALABAD 42 NIGER NIAMEY \n3 AFGANISTAN HERAT 43 NIGERIA ABUJA \n4 AFGANISTAN KABUL 44 NIGERIA LAGOS \n5 AFGANISTAN MAZAR-E-SHARIEF 45 PALESTINE GA