LAS \n \n\\ \n917. SHRI  C. MAHENDRAN \n \n Will the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any wind atlas detailing the wind resources in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether this will help the initiatives to tap the regional and local wind energy potential;      \n and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR POWER, COAL, NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND \nMINES(INDEPENDENT CHARGE) (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) & ( b): Yes Madam .The National Institute of Wind Energy (NIWE) , in September 2015, \nlaunched Wind Energy Resources Map of India at 100 meter above ground level (AGL) on \nonline Geographic Information System platform. The wind energy potential in the country at 100 \nmeter AGL is over 302 GW. This Wind Atlas is available online at NIWE websi te \nwww.niwe.res.in. \n \n (c) & (d): The Wind Resource Maps will not only help and guide the wind power \ndevelopers and other stakeholders in identifying potential areas for the development of wind \npower projects in the country but also help the central and sta te agencies in planning and \ndeveloping the infrastructure including transmission system, required for installing wind power \nprojects in these potential areas. \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |918", "t": "Development of Textile Sector", "head": "LOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.918 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF TEXTILE SECTOR \n \n918.  SHRI RABINDRA KUMAR JENA: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंरी \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken  steps to achieve sustainable growth,  modernization, \nvalue addition, increase in exports and overall development of the Textile Sector in the country; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  the details of Textile Clusters and their performance during the last five years, cluster-wise \nin the country; \n \n(d)  whether there is any proposal to establish textile cluster in Balasore district of Odisha; \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(f)  the steps taken by the Government to modernise the textile industry in the country? \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र      मंरी (श्री अज      ) \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES \n(SHRI AJAY TAMTA) \n \n(a) & (b): In order to achieve sustainable growth, modernization, value addition, increase in \nexports and for overall development of the textile sector in the country, the  Government has been \nimplementing  various policy initiatives and schemes  such as the Technolo gy Upgradation Fund \nScheme (TUFS), Schemes for the development of the Powerloom Sector, Schemes for Technical \nTextiles, Scheme for Integrated Textile Parks (SITP), Integrated Skill Development \nScheme(ISDS), Integrated Processing Development Scheme (IPDS), National Handloom \nDevelopment Progarmme (NHDP), Comprehensive Handloom Cluster Development Scheme \n(CHCDS), etc. Further, the Government has also recently launched special package for \nemployment generation and promotion of exports in Textile and Apparel sector.  \n \n(c): The details of major Textile Clusters and their performance under the Comprehensive \nCluster Development Scheme, during the last five years, cluster-wise are given in Annexure-I.  \n \n(d) & (e): Under National Handloom Development Progarmme  (NHDP),  a proposal for \nsetting up of Khaira Block Level Cluster in Balasore district has been approved and an amount of \nRs.48.52 lakh has been released out of total Government of India share of Rs.147.04 lakh to cover \n144 beneficiaries. \n \n(f): Government has taken a large number of steps through its various policy initiatives and \nschemes such as Technology Upgradation Fund Scheme (TUFS), Schemes for the development of \nthe Powerloom Sector, Schemes for Technical Textiles, Scheme for Integrated Textile Parks  \n(SITP), Integrated Skill Development Scheme(ISDS), Integrated Processing Development Scheme \n(IPDS), National Handloom Development Programme, Handloom Weavers’ Comprehensive \nWelfare Scheme, Yarn Supply Scheme, Comprehensive Handloom Cluster Development Sch eme, \netc. to provide assistance for modernization of textile industry in the country.  \n \n \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nProgress under the Comprehensive Powerloom , Handloom and Silk Cluster Development \nSchemes \n \nS.No Name of Major  \nCluster Performance/Current Status  \n1. \nErode (Tamilnadu)  \n(Announced in 2008 -\n09) \n \n(Powerloom Cluster) \nThe cluster has 60,650 looms and 94,488 weavers. \n \nThe project has been sanctioned with total project cost of Rs. 145.78 \ncrore with GOI subsidy of Rs. 40.05 crore and Promoters contribution of \nRs. 40.00 crore. GOI subsidy of Rs. 35.74 crore has been releases so far.  \n  \nSanctioned facilities :- \ni) Setting up of an Integrated Textile Market Complex which includes: \n a) weekly market,  \n b) daily market and  \n c) Exposition Hall  \nii) Warehouse and Dormitory. \n \nProgress \nC.S Associates Pvt. Ltd, Salem has been appointed Cluster Management \nand Technical Agency (CMTA) \n \nWeekly market: 1500 shops @ 48 Sq.ft per shop have been constructed \nand 419 shops are in operation.  \nDaily market: 1600 shops @ 210 Sq.ft  per shop have been constructed \nand 160 shops are in operation. \n \n2. \nBhilwara (Rajasthan)  \n(Announced in 2009 -\n10) \n(Powerloom Cluster) \nThe cluster has 16,400 looms and 43,600 weavers. \n \nKushal Global has been appointed as CMTA. \n \nDPR including the wet processing was approved in the CCG meeting held \nunder the Chairmanship of District Collector. PAMC in its meeting held on \n30.05.16 directed the SPV to submit the revised DPR as per the guidelines of \nMCPCDS. \n \n3. \nIchalkaranji \n(Maharashtra)  \n(Announced in 20 12-\n13) \n(Powerloom Cluster) \nThe cluster has 1,45,000 looms and 2,16,000 weavers. \n \nThe project has been sanctioned with total project cost of Rs. 110.51 \ncrore with GOI subsidy of Rs. 50.00 crore and Promoters contribution of \nRs. 33.33 crore. GOI subsidy of Rs. 4.11 crore has been releases so far.  \nSanctioned facilities \n \na) Warping & Sizing \nb) Process House \nc) Common Marketing Centre \n \n  \n\n \nS.No Name of Major  \nCluster \nPerformance/Current Status \n4. \nSurat (Gujarat) \nannounced in 2014 -\n2015 \n(Powerloom Cluster) \nThe cluster has 4,99,000 looms and 6,23,000 weavers \n \nSelection process of CMTA  has been completed by O/o Textile Commissioner \non 7th July 2016.  \n5. \nSolapur (Maharashtra)  \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |919", "t": "Solid Waste Management under MGNREGS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 919\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016\nSOLID WASTE MANAGEMENT UNDER MGNREGS\n919. SHRI P.K. BIJU:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is considering to provide funds under Mahatma Gandhi\nNational Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) for solid waste\nmanagement and waste collection, if so, the details thereof, State/UT -wise including\nKerala;\n(b) whether aligning of these work with MGNREGS has already begun at places in\nKerala where the State Government had accepted the proposal; and\n(c) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a): As per Schedule -I, 4 (1), IV, (i) of the Act, the solid and liquid waste management\nworks as per prescribed norms are permitted under MGNREGA. As per Schedule-I, 4 (3) of\nMahatma Gandhi NREGA, 2005, works which are non -tangible, not measurable, repetitive\nshall not be taken up under the Scheme. Works in the ambit of above mentioned, are\npermitted under the Act.\n(b)&(c): All states including the State Government of Kerala is aware of the provisions\nmentioned in the Act. Such works shall be taken up as per Section 16 of the MGNREG Act.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |920", "t": "Animal Husbandry under MGNREGS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 920\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.07.2016\nANIMAL HUSBANDRY UNDER MGNREGS\n920. SHRI ANTO ANTONY:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has received request from the Government of Kerala to\ninclude Animal Husbandry/Dairy Farming under Mahatma Gandhi National Rural\nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS); and\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the response of the Government thereto?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a): No, Madam.\n(b): Does not arise.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |921", "t": "Rashtriya Arogya Nidhi", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 921\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2016\nRASHTRIYA AROGYA NIDHI\n921. SHRI RODMAL NAGAR:\nSHRIMATI P.K. SREEMATHI TEACHER:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) the details of total number of beneficiaries under Rashtriya Arogya Nidhi along with the\nfinancial assistance provided and utilised under the scheme during the current five year plan;\n(b) whether the Government has taken steps to increase the awareness regarding the said scheme,\nif so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government proposes to implement any medical aid scheme to provide funds to\nSC/ST patients belonging to poor families;\n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor along with the steps taken in this\nregard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a): As per utilization certificates received as on date, 4305 patients have received assistance\nunder the Rashtriya Arogya Nidhi (RAN), during the current five year plan. The budgetary\nallocation and expenditure taking into account interest ac crual under RAN during the years\n2012-13 to 2016-17 are as follows:\n(Amount in Rs Lakhs)\nYear Budgetary Allocation Expenditure No. of patients benefitted*\n2012-13 1200.00 1437.33 655\n2013-14 1600.00 1593.71 1547\n2014-15 2090.00 2382.08 1344\n2015-16 2000.00 1827.87 577\n2016-17 2200.00 475.89** 182\nTotal 9090.00 7716.88 4305\n*Based on the utilization received from the hospital upto 15.07.2016.\n** Expenditure upto 15.07.2016\n\n-2-\nAllocation of funds under RAN is made to Government hospitals for treatment of\nselected life threatening diseases of patients eligible for financial assistance. State -wise\nallocations are not made under RAN.\n(b):     The various steps taken by the Government to increase awareness about the RAN Scheme,\ninter-alia, include letters to the State Governments for publicizing the Scheme in their respective\nStates, Press Note in the leading newspapers of the country giving information about the\nScheme, uploading all details regarding the Scheme, viz documents required, eligibilit y criteria,\nprocedure and contact details for queries on Ministry’s website, publication of an Appeal in the\nnewspapers disseminating information about the Scheme and circulation of Scheme details in\npamphlet format to all State Health Directors/Central Institutions and Hospitals.\n(c) & (d): No, currently there is no proposal under consideration of the Ministry. Health is a\nState Subject, The Union Government supplements the efforts of the State Government in\nmaking available health care services and i nterventions through public health facilities, with\nfocus on the marginalized and vulnerable sections of society. The public health interventions are\nguided by the extant National Health Policy which seeks to provide health care services towards\nachieving acceptable standards of good health amongst the country’s population irrespective of\ncaste, creed or religion.\n…………….."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |922", "t": "Construction of Roads in Border Areas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF DEFENCE \nLOK  SABHA \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.922 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND JULY, 2016 \n \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF ROADS IN BORDER AREAS \n \n922. SHRI  ARKA  KESHARI  DEO: \n SHRI  KAUSHALENDRA  KUMAR: \n  \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE    j{kk ea=h     \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to construct roads along borders with other \ncountries on priority basis; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the fund allocated for the purpose during 2015 -\n2016; \n \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to construct 1500 km highway along with Indo -\nChina Border in Arunachal Pradesh, if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(d)  whether the said highway will connect Tawang to Vijay Nagar on border near \nMyanmar, if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e)  the project cost and the time by which the project is likely to be completed ? \n                  \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \n \n \nMINISTER OF DEFENCE                                      (SHRI MANOHAR PARRIKAR) \n     j{kk ea=h               ¼Jh euksgj ijhZdj½ \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, Madam.  A five year (2015 -2020) Long Term Roll on Works \nPlan (LTRoWP) for upgradation / new construction of 519 roads a s \nidentified by Army has been approved for execution by Border Roads \nOrganisation (BRO) in the border regions of the country.  State wise details  \n \n……2/- \n\n:  2  : \n \n \nof the roads are enclosed as Appendix -A.  The details of fund allocated for \nthe construction / improvement of roads during 2015-16 is as under:- \n \nS. No. Agency Fund allocated \nduring 2015-16 \n(Rs. in Crores) \n \n(i) Ministry of Defence (civil) (Non Plan) 1922.00 \n(ii) Ministry of Defence (civil) (Plan) 287.80 \n(iii) Ministry of Road Transport & Highways 294.00 \n(iv) Ministry of Defence, (Capital outlay on Defence \nservices) \n \n430.00 \n(v) Ministry of Home Affairs 60.00 \n(vi) Ministry of Development of North Eastern Region 10.00 \n \n Total 3003.38 \n \n \n(c) to (e): Ministry of Home Affairs is considering the feasibility for \nconstruction of a lateral road along the Indo -China border from Dirang to \nVijaynagar in the State of Arunachal Pradesh to provide livelihood /  \nconnectivity to the border population and for the development of the \nborder area.  The road from  Tawang to Dirang already exists and is in use.  \nThe proposal is at feasibility state, due to which the project cost and \ncompletion time have not been worked out. \n \n******* \n  \n\nAPPENDIX-A  REFERRED IN THE REPLY GIVEN IN PARTS (a) & (b) OF \nLOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 922 FOR ANSWER ON \n22.7.2016 \nSTATE-WISE  DETAILS OF ROAD ENTRUSTED TO BRO IN THE \nCOUNTRY FOR UPGRADATION / NEW CONSTRUCTION : \n \nS. No. State Nos. of roads Length (in km) \n \n  1. Andaman & Nicobar 1 23.94 \n  2. Arunachal Pradesh 100 5267.51 \n  3. Assam 4 100.61 \n  4. Himachal Pradesh 11 924.52 \n  5. Jammu & Kashmir 229 7122.47 \n  6. Manipur 21 1838.00 \n  7. Mizoram 11 888.44 \n  8. Nagaland 10 841.18 \n  9. Punjab 10 276.68 \n10. Rajasthan 54 2621.02 \n11. Sikkim 37 910.17 \n12. Tripura 2 201.04 \n13. Uttarakhand 27 1117.01 \n14. West Bengal 2 92.60 \n \n Total 519 22225.17 \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |923", "t": "Ordnance Factories", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF DEFENCE PRODUCTION \nLOK  SABHA \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.923 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND JULY, 2016 \n \n \nORDNANCE  FACTORIES \n \n923. SHRI  NANA  PATOLE: \n  \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE    j{kk ea=h     \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether ordnance factories engaged in the manufacturing of arms and ammunition \nare facing stiff competition from the private companies, if so, the details the reof; \n \n(b)  whether any scheme for incentivizing the employees and officers of the ordnance \nfactories for achieving production targets within the prescribed time frame is under \nconsideration of the Government, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether any responsibility of the concerned unit head and other senior officers is \nfixed by the Government for not completing the prescribed production targets in \nordnance factories; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n                  \n           A    N    S    W    E    R \n \n \nMINISTER OF DEFENCE                                      (SHRI MANOHAR PARRIKAR) \n     j{kk ea=h                ¼Jh euksgj ijhZdj½ \n \n \n(a) Under the provisions of DPP -2016, which governs the procedure for \ncapital acquisitions, Ordnance Factories can participate in Request for \nProposal (RFPs) of Service Headquarters (SHQs) and complete with the \nIndian Private Companies, Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs) & Defence \nPublic Sector Undertakings (DPSUs) and foreign Origin al Equipment \nManufacturers (OEMs).  However, the Ministry reserves the right to \nnominate Ordnance Factory Board ( OFB) as a Production Agency (PA) in \nacquisition proposal related to its product profile. \n \n(b) No, Madam.  Ho wever, a scheme of “Piece Work ” sys tem for \nincentivizing the production and maintenance workers is being followed as \non date. \n \n….2/- \n \n\n:  2  : \n \n \n(c) & (d): The targets assigned to the factories are generally met.  \nSometimes the units fail to achieve the target on account of various \nreasons.  They are:- \n \n(i) Delay in availability of covering indent. \n(ii) Non availability of material due to delay in financial clearance by \nthe Competent Financial Authority (CFA).  \n \n(iii) Stoppage of production by User / Directorate General of Quality \nAssurance (DGQA) during the middle of the financial year. \n \n(iv) Unforeseen / Unavoidable Machine Breakdown.  \n \n \nPerformance of concerned Unit Head and other concerned Senior \nOfficers vis-à-vis production targets is recorded by way of appropriate \ngrading in the Annual Performance Appraisal Report. \n \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|9|UNSTARRED |924", "t": "Integration of Staff Under NHM", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 924\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2016\nINTEGRATION OF STAFF UNDER NHM\n924. SHRI SUMAN BALKA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to integrate its staff under National Health Mission (NHM)\nworking on various programmes including Revised National Tuberculosis Control programme\n(RNTCP), National Vect or Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP) and Reproductive\nChild Health (RCH) under single umbrella to check duplication of staff and opening up round\nthe clock service; and\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a) & (b):     The Mission Steering Group (MSG) of National Health Mission (NHM) in its\nmeeting held on 29 th March 2016 has approved for applying health system approach to Human\nResource (HR) under NHM.  The gist of the decisions is as under:\n Optimize utilization of HR and avoid duplication by bringing all facility based service -\ndelivery HR together and applying health systems approach as opposed to programmatic\napproach.\n Implement Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS) and workload as the basis to determine the\nnumber of HR required and for considering any additional human resource including\nprogramme management posts.\n Apply similar norms of performance appraisal and increment for all contractual HR.\n Bring parity in remuneration for posts under the NHM in the particular State Health Society\ncommensurate with qualifications and experience.\nLetter from AS&MD (NHM) has 